# `lst.no_such_method()` reached Roslyn and came back as CS1061 behind SPY0908 — the compiler
# reporting its own bug for a typo, on the three receivers Python programs use most (#1344).
#
# The BuiltinType-only gate that #1291 left behind was not an omission: a container member is
# reached by spellings the #1141 absence proof did not model, and refusing one of those rejects a
# working program. The proof was taught those spellings first (see the twin fixture, which pins
# every one of them); only then did the gate widen to a container receiver whose own constructed
# CLR type reflection can see.


def main() -> None:
    lst: list[int] = [1, 2, 3]
    print(lst.no_such_method())

    d: dict[str, int] = {"a": 1}
    print(d.no_such_method())

    st: set[int] = {1, 2}
    print(st.no_such_method())
